<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Philippine Commission</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1899">1899</a>–<a href="/years/1916">1916</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Philippine Commission was a body appointed by the President of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> to govern the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philippines">Philippines</a>. The second commission, under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-howard-taft">William Howard Taft</a>, took over civil government from the army in 1901 and acted as the legislature, later as the upper house beside the elected Philippine Assembly from 1907. The Jones Law of 1916 replaced it with an elected Senate.</p></div>
